34. Find First and Last Position of Element in Sorted Array

Given an array of integers nums sorted in non-decreasing order, find the starting and ending position of a given target value.
If target is not found in the array, return [-1, -1].
You must write an algorithm with O(log n) runtime complexity.

Explanation:

🎯 What are we trying to find?
For searchRange we want two different things:
1️⃣ First occurrence of target
2️⃣ Last occurrence of target

These are two different goals.
🔍 Normal binary search mindset -> Binary search normally answers only this question:
            ❓ Does the target exist?
            Once you find it, you stop.
            But here we want positions, not existence.

🧠 Key idea (MOST IMPORTANT)
When nums[mid] == target, mid is a valid answer, but it may not be the first or last one.
So you save mid and keep searching.

🟦 Case 1: Finding the FIRST occurrence
Question we ask:
“Could there be another target before this index?”
Example
[5, 7, 7, 8, 8, 10]
           ↑
          mid = 4 (value = 8)

Is this the first 8?
❌ No — there is another 8 at index 3  👉 So we must search LEFT
That’s why:
right = mid - 1;   // move LEFT

We already stored mid as a possible answer.
Now we discard the right half and check if there’s an earlier one.

🟩 Case 2: Finding the LAST occurrence
Question we ask: “Could there be another target after this index?”

Same example
[5, 7, 7, 8, 8, 10]
          ↑
      mid = 3 (value = 8)

Is this the last 8? ❌ No — there is another 8 at index 4 👉 So we must search RIGHT
That’s why: left = mid + 1;   // move RIGHT

We already stored mid as a possible answer.
Now we discard the left half and look for a later one.


Code:
  class Solution {
    public int[] searchRange(int[] nums, int target) {
        int[] arr= {-1,-1};
        int n=nums.length;

        if(n==0){
            return arr;
        }

        int left=0; int right=n-1;
        while(left<=right){
            int mid=(left+right)/2;
            if(nums[mid]==target){
                arr[0]=mid;
                right=mid-1;
            }
            else if(nums[mid]>target){
                right=mid-1;
            }
            else if(nums[mid]<target){
                left=mid+1;
            }
        }

         left=0;  right=n-1;
        while(left<=right){
            int mid=(left+right)/2;
            if(nums[mid]==target){
                arr[1]=mid;
                left=mid+1;
            }
            else if(nums[mid]>target){
                right=mid-1;
            }
            else if(nums[mid]<target){
                left=mid+1;
            }
        }
        return arr;
    }
}
